<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Agaw people</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Agaw are a Cushitic-speaking people of the northern and central highlands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eritrea">Eritrea</a>, among the oldest inhabitants of the region. An Agaw dynasty, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zagwe-dynasty">Zagwe</a>, ruled Ethiopia from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lasta">Lasta</a> from about the 12th century until 1270 and built the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rock-hewn-churches-of-lalibela">rock-hewn churches</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lalibela">Lalibela</a>. Agaw languages survive in several communities, though many Agaw have shifted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amharic">Amharic</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigrinya-language">Tigrinya</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/agaw-people.jpg" alt="Agaw people" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Abrham Zelalem · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Injibara-az-0002.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Agaw speak the Agaw languages, also called the Central Cushitic languages, a part of the Cushitic branch of the Afroasiatic family. They are first mentioned in the Monumentum Adulitanum, an Aksumite inscription of the third century recorded in the sixth century by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cosmas-indicopleustes">Cosmas Indicopleustes</a>, which names a people called Athagaus, perhaps from ʿAd Agaw, meaning sons of Agaw, among those conquered by its unnamed king.</p><p>They appear again as the Atagaw in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ezana-stone">Ezana Stone</a> of the fourth century. Cosmas also noted that a major <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gold-trade">gold trade</a> route passed through the region of Agau, west of the Tekezé River and south of the Semien Mountains, and that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaleb">King Kaleb</a> of Axum had a governor there to protect the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caravan-trade">caravan routes</a>. According to Taddesse Tamrat, this governor probably had his seat in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lasta">Lasta</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Agaw formed and ruled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zagwe-dynasty">Zagwe dynasty</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>, from about 1137 to 1270. Later sources accused the Zagwe of being usurpers and belittled their achievements. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gebre-mesqel-lalibela">Gebre Mesqel Lalibela</a>, a Zagwe ruler, is credited with building the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rock-hewn-churches-of-lalibela">rock-hewn churches</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lalibela">Lalibela</a>. The Zagwe were deposed by a Semitic-speaking Amhara dynasty, which claimed to resume the Solomonic lineage of the earlier Aksumite kingdom, though it gave the Zagwe rulers and their descendants the title of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wagshum">Wagshum</a> so that they could govern their native regions of Wag and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lasta">Lasta</a>.</p><p>Travellers and outside observers noted that Agaw peoples practised what some called a Hebraic religion, while some followed Ethiopian Orthodoxy and many were Beta Israel Jews. Thousands of Agaw Beta Israel converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> in the 19th and early 20th centuries, both voluntarily and under force, and became the Falash Mura.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The Agaw consist of several linguistic groups living in scattered communities from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eritrea">Eritrea</a> to Gojjam, and local traditions point to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lasta">Lasta</a> as the place from which they dispersed. The Northern Agaw are the Bilen, around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/keren">Keren</a> in Eritrea; the Western Agaw are the Qemant, around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gondar">Gondar</a>; the Eastern Agaw are the Xamir; and the Southern Agaw are the Awi, who live around Dangila south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-tana">Lake Tana</a>.</p><p>Many Agaw also speak <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amharic">Amharic</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigrinya-language">Tigrinya</a> or Tigre, and Amharic has influenced their languages. The Agaw varieties differ among themselves more than the Romance languages do, while Amharic speakers differ little, which points to a recent spread of Ethio-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/semitic-languages">Semitic languages</a> through the Agaw homeland.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Agaw people” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After the decline of the Kingdom of Aksum, power shifted south to the <b>Agaw people</b> of Lasta.</blockquote><a href="/state/zagwe-dynasty">Zagwe dynasty</a></li></ul></section>
